1
00:00:00,268 --> 00:00:03,086
From 75% to 7.5%…

2
00:00:03,192 --> 00:00:04,595
on the same kind of exam.

3
00:00:04,784 --> 00:00:05,444
What happened?

4
00:00:05,852 --> 00:00:07,243
It's called contamination:

5
00:00:07,385 --> 00:00:09,333
the exam questions leak into the data

6
00:00:09,343 --> 00:00:10,651
used to train the model.

7
00:00:11,064 --> 00:00:12,717
It's like a student who's already seen

8
00:00:12,727 --> 00:00:13,458
the exam:

9
00:00:13,647 --> 00:00:14,934
you can't tell if they understand the

10
00:00:14,944 --> 00:00:15,639
subject…

11
00:00:15,734 --> 00:00:17,042
or just know the answers.

12
00:00:17,452 --> 00:00:19,162
And it happens more than you think:

13
00:00:19,386 --> 00:00:22,041
surveys report up to 45% of test

14
00:00:22,051 --> 00:00:23,914
material leaked into training data.

15
00:00:24,344 --> 00:00:26,421
That's why the Konwinski Prize only uses

16
00:00:26,431 --> 00:00:29,120
GitHub issues filed after the training cutoff.

17
00:00:29,309 --> 00:00:30,594
Impossible to have seen them.

18
00:00:30,783 --> 00:00:31,360
The winner:

19
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:32,952
7.5%.

20
00:00:33,384 --> 00:00:34,044
The lesson:

21
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:36,098
a saturated exam is fixed with a

22
00:00:36,108 --> 00:00:36,898
harder one.

23
00:00:37,087 --> 00:00:39,435
A contaminated one, only with an exam

24
00:00:39,445 --> 00:00:40,907
the model has never seen.

25
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:43,218
So when you see a spectacular score,

26
00:00:43,419 --> 00:00:43,997
ask:

27
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:45,402
could this exam have been in its

28
00:00:45,412 --> 00:00:46,237
training data?

29
00:00:46,664 --> 00:00:49,152
Because a high score isn't always intelligence.

30
00:00:49,353 --> 00:00:50,661
Sometimes, it's memory.

31
00:00:51,072 --> 00:00:52,631
Save it, and share it with someone

32
00:00:52,641 --> 00:00:54,409
who believes every leaderboard.

